{"hash":"7779ec0fa1e93813","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":92.935,"said":"You have stepped up onto the temple’s plinth. Look up: three crowns rise from one body, a tall spire over the sanctum, a dome over the hall, and a third spire of plain stone. The temple you see was built in 1780 by Ahilyabai Holkar. Who was she? A queen of the Holkar house of Indore, who governed her kingdom from Maheshwar on the Narmada for nearly thirty years, and who spent her wealth on pilgrims: ghats, wells, rest houses and temples, all across India. A few years later she raised a temple for worship at Somnath too. Here she gave Vishwanath a home again, beside the old site, and the worship has gone on in it ever since. Half a century later came a second gift. Maharaja Ranjit Singh, the Sikh ruler of Punjab, gave gold, and in the 1830s the spire and the dome were covered with it; that is why many call this the Golden Temple of Kashi. Look closely: of the three crowns, only two are gold. The temple trust itself notes that the third is still bare stone. Before you is the sanctum’s doorway, framed in silver. Take a breath, and step inside with me.\n\nDid you know? In 1785 a Naubatkhana, a drum house for temple music, was built in front of the temple, the trust records. Since 1983 the temple has been run by a trust under the Uttar Pradesh government.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1064},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1080,"end":1268}],"words":[[0.098,0.168,0,3],[0.278,0.18,4,8],[0.471,0.385,9,16],[0.867,0.24,17,19],[1.12,0.3,20,24],[1.432,0.108,25,28],[1.552,0.541,29,37],[2.105,0.445,38,44],[3.474,0.24,46,50],[3.726,0.24,51,53],[4.195,0.252,55,60],[4.459,0.445,61,67],[4.916,0.312,68,72],[5.24,0.24,73,77],[5.493,0.252,78,81],[5.757,0.493,82,86],[6.526,0.084,88,89],[6.623,0.312,90,94],[6.947,0.517,95,100],[7.476,0.204,101,105],[7.692,0.084,106,109],[7.788,0.613,110,117],[8.666,0.096,119,120],[8.774,0.349,121,125],[9.135,0.24,126,130],[9.387,0.084,131,134],[9.48